Pensions buy corporate bonds and private credit, then futures for long-rate sway;
hedge funds buy Treasuries, sell those futures, and pocket the basis spread as pay.
“Duration” means how strongly bond prices respond when interest rates change;
managers choose a target and expect their futures to keep it in range.

